1 GOTO

   Goes on at a labeled command of the command procedure.

   Format

     GOTO label

2 Parameter

 label

   A label, a name followed by a colon at the start of a command line,
   alone or before a command:

       $ LOOP:
       $ COUNT = COUNT + 1
       $ IF COUNT .LT. 10 THEN GOTO LOOP

2 Description

   A label is known once the procedure has run past it.  GOTO takes the
   one run past last, and otherwise searches forward for the first one.
   A label inside an IF block or a subroutine (see CALL) that the GOTO
   isn't in can't be gone to, and a GOTO out of an IF block ends the
   block.  When the label isn't found, GOTO reports it (DCL-W-USGOTO),
   and the procedure ends.

   Labels belong to their command level: a procedure, or a CALL
   subroutine.  At the terminal a label is ignored (DCL-W-NOLBLS).  GOTO
   leaves $STATUS as it was.

2 Examples

   1.$ IF P1 .EQS. "" THEN GOTO USAGE
     $ ...
     $ USAGE:
     $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "A file name is needed"

     Goes to USAGE when no parameter was given.
